{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed720058768/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a32d8b75\" lines=\"262-275\">\n<description>\nAssemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.\n- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.\n\nRule:\n- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.\n- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).\n- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.\n\nKey insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7ed72f31\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.\n- Colored sprites appear near red markers.\n\nRule:\n- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.\n- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.\n- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.\n- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.\n\nKey insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"35ab12c3\" lines=\"73-88\">\n<description>\nComplete outlined shapes and carry attached shadow pixels along their edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Each active color marks vertices of an 8-direction aligned outline.\n- A start vertex can have adjacent non-bg, different-colored shadow pixels.\n- Other colored pixels may be distractors unless they lie on valid drawable edges.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, order its pixels by polar angle around their centroid to form a cycle.\n- Draw only horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree edges whose interiors are clear.\n- From the chosen start vertex, copy adjacent shadow offsets along each drawable edge\n  when the parallel path is in bounds and clear.\n\nKey insight: The main outline is reconstructed first, then the start vertex's neighbor offsets define parallel shadow strokes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b9c3697\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nInsert red components into interior sockets of large structures, leaving a black trail.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common. Large non-bg, non-red structures with interior cavities.\n- Red components outside structures.\n\nRule:\n- Interior sockets: bg cells inside a structure's bbox, adjacent to structure pixels.\n- For each structure, find the direction where rays from sockets hit a red component.\n- Slide that component into the socket; paint traversed cells black.\n- Unmatched reds vanish.\n\nKey insight: Docking direction is inferred by ray-casting from interior sockets to the nearest red component.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b5033c1\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nStraighten a colored snake path into a single-column strip preserving pixel order.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Non-bg pixels form a single 4-connected path (snake) with exactly two endpoints.\n- Each path pixel may be a different non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two endpoints (cells with only one path neighbor) of the snake.\n- Begin at the top-left-most endpoint and trace the path cell by cell.\n- Output an (N×1) column listing each cell's color in traversal order.\n\nKey insight: The path is a simple chain with no branches; choosing the top-left-most endpoint fixes a unique reading direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f931b4a8\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nCount the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.\n- Black is the background.\n- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.\n- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.\n\nRule:\n- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.\n- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.\n- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.\n- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.\n\nKey insight:\n- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16de56c4\" lines=\"9-34\">\n<description>\nFill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.\n- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,\n  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).\n- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;\n  their gap defines the step.\n- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a\n  repeating color.\n\nRule:\n- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.\n- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;\n  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.\n- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
