e a grid and paste the legend away from all canvas edges.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval/seed1699747228/_codegen_logs/retry_5_5.log:2467:RS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+- fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+- identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\nexec\\n/bin/bash -lc \\\"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python - <<'PY'\\nimport importlib.util\\nfrom collections import Counter\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\\n\\nsamples = 100\\nlegacy_diff = 0\\npartial_totals = []\\nmax_depths = []\\nframe_totals = []\\nrepeat_excesses = []\\nfor _ in range(samples):\\n    inp = mod.generate_input()\\n    squares = mod._detect_squares(inp)\\n    azure = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s['c0']))\\n    red = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.RED), key=lambda s: -int(s['c0']))\\n    profile = mod._behavior_profile(azure, red, int(mod.get_bg(inp)))\\n    if not np.array_equal(profile['fused_output'], profile['legacy_output']):\\n        legacy_diff += 1\\n    partial_totals.append(int(profile['azure_partial_pairs']) + int(profile['red_partial_pairs']))\\n    max_depths.append(max(int(profile['azure_max_depth']), int(profile['red_max_depth'])))\\n    frame_totals.append(len(squares))\\n    counts = Counter([int(s['r0']) for s in azure] + [int(s['r0']) for s in red])\\n    repeat_excesses.append(sum(v - 1 for v in counts.values() if v > 1))\\n\\nprint('samples', samples)\\nprint('legacy_diff_rate', legacy_diff, '/', samples)\\nprint('frame_total_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(frame_totals).items())))\\nprint('max_depth_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(max_depths).items())))\\nprint('partial_pair_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(partial_totals).items())))\\nprint('repeat_excess_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(repeat_excesses).items())))\\nprint('avg_partial_pairs', round(sum(partial_totals)/samples, 2))\\nPY\\\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cecae3f8687e74c9fc61259fa8649238da2120f2\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,368 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+- fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+- identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\n succeeded in 2086ms:\\nsamples 100\\nlegacy_diff_rate 100 / 100\\nframe_total_dist {10: 30, 11: 46, 12: 24}\\nmax_depth_dist {3: 53, 4: 47}\\npartial_pair_dist {6: 9, 7: 26, 8: 33, 9: 15, 10: 8, 11: 5, 12: 2, 14: 1, 16: 1}\\nrepeat_excess_dist {4: 30, 5: 46, 6: 24}\\navg_partial_pairs 8.24\\n\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cecae3f8687e74c9fc61259fa8649238da2120f2\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,368 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+- includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+- side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+- fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+- identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (29, 24, 19, 14, 9, 4)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    counts = Counter((int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\ncodex\\n我发现一个可以顺手提高稳健性的点：把输入列间距从 5 改成 6，可以保留 1 格空隙，降低 5x5 滑窗误检风险，同时不影响层叠规则。我会补这个小修正，再复跑测试。\\napply patch\\npatch: completed\\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+  fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+  identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\\n+                return None\\n+\\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+  fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+  identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\\n+                return None\\n+\\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\nexec\\n/bin/bash -lc '/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python -m py_compile /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py' in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\\n succeeded in 0ms:\\nexec\\n/bin/bash -lc \\\"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python -c \\\\\\\"import numpy as np, importlib.util, time, sys\\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\\nok, fail = 0, 0\\nt0 = time.time()\\nfor i in range(50):\\n    try:\\n        inp = mod.generate_input()\\n        out = mod.main(inp)\\n        ok += 1\\n    except Exception as e:\\n        fail += 1\\n        if fail <= 3: print(f'Trial {i}: {e}')\\nelapsed = time.time() - t0\\nprint(f'Success: {ok}/50, Time: {elapsed:.1f}s ({elapsed/50*1000:.0f}ms/call)')\\nif ok < 40: sys.exit(1)\\n\\\\\\\"\\\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+  fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+  identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\\n+                return None\\n+\\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\n succeeded in 1437ms:\\nSuccess: 50/50, Time: 1.2s (23ms/call)\\n\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+  fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+  identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\\n+                return None\\n+\\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\nexec\\n/bin/bash -lc \\\"/home/xd/miniconda3/bin/python - <<'PY'\\nimport importlib.util\\nfrom collections import Counter\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('variant', '/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py')\\nmod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\\nspec.loader.exec_module(mod)\\n\\nsamples = 100\\nlegacy_diff = 0\\npartial_totals = []\\nmax_depths = []\\nframe_totals = []\\nrepeat_excesses = []\\nfor _ in range(samples):\\n    inp = mod.generate_input()\\n    squares = mod._detect_squares(inp)\\n    azure = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s['c0']))\\n    red = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s['frame']) == mod.RED), key=lambda s: -int(s['c0']))\\n    profile = mod._behavior_profile(azure, red, int(mod.get_bg(inp)))\\n    if not np.array_equal(profile['fused_output'], profile['legacy_output']):\\n        legacy_diff += 1\\n    partial_totals.append(int(profile['azure_partial_pairs']) + int(profile['red_partial_pairs']))\\n    max_depths.append(max(int(profile['azure_max_depth']), int(profile['red_max_depth'])))\\n    frame_totals.append(len(squares))\\n    counts = Counter([int(s['r0']) for s in azure] + [int(s['r0']) for s in red])\\n    repeat_excesses.append(sum(v - 1 for v in counts.values() if v > 1))\\n\\nprint('samples', samples)\\nprint('legacy_diff_rate', legacy_diff, '/', samples)\\nprint('frame_total_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(frame_totals).items())))\\nprint('max_depth_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(max_depths).items())))\\nprint('partial_pair_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(partial_totals).items())))\\nprint('repeat_excess_dist', dict(sorted(Counter(repeat_excesses).items())))\\nprint('avg_partial_pairs', round(sum(partial_totals)/samples, 2))\\nPY\\\" in /home/lixinyu/snap/new\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames overlap even when they share the same row.\\n+- Output placement is also in-bounds because the solver only uses up to 4 layers per\\n+  side on a 42-column canvas.\\n+\\n+e) Visual observability\\n+- A human can see two distinct cues in the examples:\\n+  1. many frames of each border color still migrate to their designated side;\\n+  2. some same-color frames with identical top rows form deep horizontal stacks,\\n+     while another same-color frame with a visibly different top row but overlapping\\n+     height stays in the edge column and partly covers the earlier one.\\n+- No color-id arithmetic or hidden metadata is used. The changed behavior depends only\\n+  on visible row relationships and visible input-column order.\\n+\\n+f) Visual cleanliness\\n+- The generator limits the deliberate partial-overlap occlusion to one compact cluster\\n+  per side, and keeps the two colors in separated vertical bands. This preserves the\\n+  intended \\\"messier than base task\\\" look without turning the whole output into an\\n+  unreadable pile of overdraw.\\n+\\n+g) Change inferability\\n+- There is no new recoloring or symbolic remapping. Inner colors remain unique within\\n+  each border color so each frame is trackable across input and output.\\n+- The key fused contrast is structural: exact row repetition creates extra depth,\\n+  partial overlap does not. The generator rejects samples where the fused output would\\n+  match a generalized legacy \\\"any overlap stacks inward\\\" output, so the D11 change is\\n+  always active rather than accidental.\\n+\\n+9) New assumptions introduced and generator guarantees\\n+- Assumption: at least one partial-overlap pair exists. Guarantee: each side pattern\\n+  includes one row offset by 2 or 3 from the repeated anchor row.\\n+- Assumption: multi-layer depth is visibly larger than the base task. Guarantee: each\\n+  side has at least 3 layers, and some samples reach 4.\\n+- Assumption: the new containment predicate matters. Guarantee: generation compares the\\n+  fused output against an \\\"any overlap\\\" baseline and rejects any sample where they are\\n+  identical.\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+\\n+\\n+SQ = 5\\n+AZURE = 8\\n+RED = 2\\n+GRID_H = 30\\n+GRID_W = 42\\n+N_AZURE_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+N_RED_RANGE = (5, 6)\\n+MAX_LAYERS = 4\\n+GEN_RETRIES = 400\\n+\\n+AZURE_INPUT_COLS = (7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37)\\n+RED_INPUT_COLS = (30, 24, 18, 12, 6, 0)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _overlaps(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return not (int(a1) < int(b0) or int(b1) < int(a0))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fully_nested(a0: int, a1: int, b0: int, b1: int) -> bool:\\n+    return (int(a0) <= int(b0) and int(b1) <= int(a1)) or (int(b0) <= int(a0) and int(a1) <= int(b1))\\n+\\n+\\n+def _make_patch(frame_color: int, inner_color: int) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    patch = np.full((SQ, SQ), int(frame_color), dtype=int)\\n+    patch[1:-1, 1:-1] = int(inner_color)\\n+    return patch\\n+\\n+\\n+def _rectangles_overlap(a: tuple[int, int], b: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\\n+    return abs(int(a[0]) - int(b[0])) < SQ and abs(int(a[1]) - int(b[1])) < SQ\\n+\\n+\\n+def _detect_squares(grid: np.ndarray) -> list[dict]:\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    squares: list[dict] = []\\n+    for r0 in range(h - SQ + 1):\\n+        for c0 in range(w - SQ + 1):\\n+            patch = crop_bbox(g, (int(r0), int(c0), int(r0 + SQ - 1), int(c0 + SQ - 1)))\\n+            border = np.concatenate([patch[0, :], patch[-1, :], patch[1:-1, 0], patch[1:-1, -1]])\\n+            if int(border.min()) != int(border.max()):\\n+                continue\\n+            frame_col = int(border[0])\\n+            if frame_col not in {AZURE, RED}:\\n+                continue\\n+            inner = patch[1:-1, 1:-1]\\n+            if not bool((inner != int(frame_col)).any()):\\n+                continue\\n+            squares.append({\\\"frame\\\": frame_col, \\\"r0\\\": int(r0), \\\"c0\\\": int(c0), \\\"patch\\\": patch})\\n+    return squares\\n+\\n+\\n+def _stack_side(\\n+    ordered_squares: list[dict],\\n+    width: int,\\n+    edge_left: bool,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> list[dict]:\\n+    placed_rows_by_layer: list[list[tuple[int, int]]] = []\\n+    placements: list[dict] = []\\n+    for square in ordered_squares:\\n+        r0 = int(square[\\\"r0\\\"])\\n+        r1 = int(r0 + SQ - 1)\\n+        layer = None\\n+        for idx, intervals in enumerate(placed_rows_by_layer):\\n+            if any(conflict_fn(r0, r1, pr0, pr1) for pr0, pr1 in intervals):\\n+                continue\\n+            layer = int(idx)\\n+            break\\n+        if layer is None:\\n+            layer = len(placed_rows_by_layer)\\n+            placed_rows_by_layer.append([])\\n+        placed_rows_by_layer[layer].append((r0, r1))\\n+        dest_c = int(layer * SQ) if edge_left else int(width - SQ * (layer + 1))\\n+        assert 0 <= dest_c <= width - SQ, \\\"stack depth exceeds canvas width\\\"\\n+        placements.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(square[\\\"frame\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": r0,\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]),\\n+                \\\"dest_c\\\": dest_c,\\n+                \\\"layer\\\": layer,\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": square[\\\"patch\\\"],\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return placements\\n+\\n+\\n+def _render_from_ordered_sides(\\n+    bg: int,\\n+    azure_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    red_ordered: list[dict],\\n+    *,\\n+    conflict_fn,\\n+) -> tuple[np.ndarray, dict]:\\n+    out = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    azure_placed = _stack_side(azure_ordered, GRID_W, True, conflict_fn)\\n+    red_placed = _stack_side(red_ordered, GRID_W, False, conflict_fn)\\n+    for item in azure_placed + red_placed:\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+\\n+    metrics = {\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in azure_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": max((int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) + 1 for item in red_placed), default=0),\\n+        \\\"azure_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in azure_placed],\\n+        \\\"red_layers\\\": [int(item[\\\"layer\\\"]) for item in red_placed],\\n+    }\\n+    return out, metrics\\n+\\n+\\n+def _count_partial_pairs(ordered_squares: list[dict]) -> int:\\n+    rows = [int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]) for square in ordered_squares]\\n+    count = 0\\n+    for i in range(len(rows)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(rows)):\\n+            a0 = int(rows[i])\\n+            b0 = int(rows[j])\\n+            if _overlaps(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1) and not _fully_nested(a0, a0 + SQ - 1, b0, b0 + SQ - 1):\\n+                count += 1\\n+    return count\\n+\\n+\\n+def _behavior_profile(azure_ordered: list[dict], red_ordered: list[dict], bg: int) -> dict:\\n+    fused_out, fused_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_fully_nested)\\n+    legacy_out, legacy_metrics = _render_from_ordered_sides(bg, azure_ordered, red_ordered, conflict_fn=_overlaps)\\n+    return {\\n+        \\\"fused_output\\\": fused_out,\\n+        \\\"legacy_output\\\": legacy_out,\\n+        \\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(azure_ordered),\\n+        \\\"red_partial_pairs\\\": _count_partial_pairs(red_ordered),\\n+        \\\"azure_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"red_max_depth\\\": int(fused_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_azure_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+        \\\"legacy_red_max_depth\\\": int(legacy_metrics[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"]),\\n+    }\\n+\\n+\\n+def _sample_side_rows(n: int, top_band: bool) -> list[int]:\\n+    if top_band:\\n+        base = random.randint(0, 2)\\n+        far = random.randint(8, 10)\\n+    else:\\n+        base = random.randint(15, 17)\\n+        far = random.randint(21, 23)\\n+    overlap_row = int(base + random.choice((2, 3)))\\n+    patterns = {\\n+        5: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+        6: [\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, base, far, base],\\n+            [base, overlap_row, base, far, base, far],\\n+        ],\\n+    }\\n+    rows = list(random.choice(patterns[int(n)]))\\n+    assert all(0 <= int(r) <= GRID_H - SQ for r in rows)\\n+    return rows\\n+\\n+\\n+def _ordered_side_squares(rows: list[int], cols: tuple[int, ...], frame_color: int, colors: list[int]) -> list[dict]:\\n+    ordered: list[dict] = []\\n+    for row, col, inner_color in zip(rows, cols[: len(rows)], colors):\\n+        ordered.append(\\n+            {\\n+                \\\"frame\\\": int(frame_color),\\n+                \\\"r0\\\": int(row),\\n+                \\\"c0\\\": int(col),\\n+                \\\"patch\\\": _make_patch(int(frame_color), int(inner_color)),\\n+            }\\n+        )\\n+    return ordered\\n+\\n+\\n+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    Re-stack many bordered 5x5 frames onto their color-designated edges.\\n+\\n+    Input structure:\\n+    - Background is the most common color.\\n+    - The grid contains 10-12 non-overlapping 5x5 framed squares.\\n+    - Teal (8) and red (2) are the only frame colors; each frame has a uniform 3x3 inner fill.\\n+\\n+    Rule:\\n+    - Teal frames are processed by increasing input column and stacked from the left edge.\\n+    - Red frames are processed by decreasing input column and stacked from the right edge.\\n+    - Each frame keeps its original top row.\\n+    - A frame moves one more square-width inward only when its 5-row span is fully contained\\n+      in a previously used layer on that same side; partial row overlap does not force a shift.\\n+\\n+    Key insight:\\n+    - With fixed 5x5 frames, \\\"full row containment\\\" means matching the same row span, so exact\\n+      row repeats create deeper layers while merely partial overlaps stay in place and can overdraw.\\n+    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\\n+    h, w = g.shape\\n+    bg = int(get_bg(g))\\n+    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\\n+\\n+    squares = _detect_squares(g)\\n+    azure_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == AZURE), key=lambda s: int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+    red_ordered = sorted((s for s in squares if int(s[\\\"frame\\\"]) == RED), key=lambda s: -int(s[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    for item in _stack_side(azure_ordered, w, True, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    for item in _stack_side(red_ordered, w, False, _fully_nested):\\n+        paste(out, item[\\\"patch\\\"], int(item[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(item[\\\"dest_c\\\"]), transparent=bg)\\n+    return out\\n+\\n+\\n+def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\\n+    bg = random.choice([color for color in range(10) if color not in {AZURE, RED}])\\n+    n_azure = random.randint(*N_AZURE_RANGE)\\n+    n_red = random.randint(*N_RED_RANGE)\\n+\\n+    azure_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_azure, top_band=True)\\n+    red_rows = _sample_side_rows(n_red, top_band=False)\\n+\\n+    azure_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, AZURE}]\\n+    red_choices = [color for color in range(10) if color not in {bg, RED}]\\n+    if len(azure_choices) < n_azure or len(red_choices) < n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+    azure_colors = random.sample(azure_choices, n_azure)\\n+    red_colors = random.sample(red_choices, n_red)\\n+\\n+    azure_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(azure_rows, AZURE_INPUT_COLS, AZURE, azure_colors)\\n+    red_ordered = _ordered_side_squares(red_rows, RED_INPUT_COLS, RED, red_colors)\\n+\\n+    profile = _behavior_profile(azure_ordered, red_ordered, bg)\\n+    if profile[\\\"azure_partial_pairs\\\"] + profile[\\\"red_partial_pairs\\\"] < 2:\\n+        return None\\n+    if max(int(profile[\\\"azure_max_depth\\\"]), int(profile[\\\"red_max_depth\\\"])) < 3:\\n+        return None\\n+    if np.array_equal(profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"], profile[\\\"legacy_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    inp = np.full((GRID_H, GRID_W), int(bg), dtype=int)\\n+    for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered:\\n+        paste(inp, square[\\\"patch\\\"], int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"]))\\n+\\n+    top_lefts = [(int(square[\\\"r0\\\"]), int(square[\\\"c0\\\"])) for square in azure_ordered + red_ordered]\\n+    counts = Counter(top_lefts)\\n+    if counts and max(counts.values()) > 1:\\n+        return None\\n+    for i in range(len(top_lefts)):\\n+        for j in range(i + 1, len(top_lefts)):\\n+            if _rectangles_overlap(top_lefts[i], top_lefts[j]):\\n+                return None\\n+\\n+    detected = _detect_squares(inp)\\n+    if len(detected) != n_azure + n_red:\\n+        return None\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        out = main(inp)\\n+    except Exception:\\n+        return None\\n+    if not np.array_equal(out, profile[\\\"fused_output\\\"]):\\n+        return None\\n+    return inp\\n+\\n+\\n+def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\\n+    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\\n+        result = _gen_attempt()\\n+        if result is not None:\\n+            return result\\n+    raise RuntimeError(f\\\"Failed to generate valid input after {GEN_RETRIES} attempts\\\")\\n\\n succeeded in 2631ms:\\nsamples 100\\nlegacy_diff_rate 100 / 100\\nframe_total_dist {10: 26, 11: 54, 12: 20}\\nmax_depth_dist {3: 50, 4: 50}\\npartial_pair_dist {6: 5, 7: 29, 8: 32, 9: 18, 10: 5, 11: 1, 12: 1, 13: 2, 14: 5, 15: 2}\\nrepeat_excess_dist {4: 26, 5: 54, 6: 20}\\navg_partial_pairs 8.5\\n\\ndiff --git a/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f6ecd3ed4a6a7e1aaf79eb74ab58fd13d37129d9\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/ceshi/code_combo/b5ca7ac4/12/code.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,381 @@\\n+# Combo variant of b5ca7ac4 (index 12)\\n+# combo_spec: D11/0+D12/2\\n+# Name: 大量框架+完全包含才偏移\\n+\\n+from __future__ import annotations\\n+\\n+import random\\n+import sys\\n+from collections import Counter\\n+from pathlib import Path\\n+\\n+import numpy as np\\n+\\n+_SOLVERS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4] / \\\"solvers\\\"\\n+if str(_SOLVERS_DIR) not in sys.path:\\n+    sys.path.append(str(_SOLVERS_DIR))\\n+\\n+from arc_utils import crop_bbox, get_bg, paste\\n+\\n+\\n+\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n+Design analysis for combo D11/0 + D12/2\\n+\\n+a) Concrete behavior and trace\\n+- The original solver moves every teal frame to the left side and every red frame to\\n+  the right side, preserving rows and using input-column order within each color.\\n+  Any row overlap with an already edge-placed same-color frame forces a one-step\\n+  horizontal offset, so the original generator only needs two layers.\\n+- In the fused variant, the frame count rises to 10-12 total and the overlap test is\\n+  stricter: a shift happens only when one 5-row span fully contains the other.\\n+- Because every frame is still exactly 5x5, \\\"full containment\\\" between row spans\\n+  collapses to equality of the row start. Example for teal processing order:\\n+  rows `[1, 3, 1, 1, 9, 1]`.\\n+  Under the old rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer1` because rows 1..5 and 3..7 overlap,\\n+  then later `1`s are forced even farther inward by overlap checks.\\n+  Under the fused rule:\\n+  `1 -> layer0`, `3 -> layer0` because partial overlap is ignored,\\n+  next `1 -> layer1`, next `1 -> layer2`, `9 -> layer0`, final `1 -> layer3`.\\n+  The visible result is a deep 4-column stack for the repeated exact row, plus one\\n+  partially overlapping frame that stays on the edge and visibly occludes part of the\\n+  first edge frame instead of being shifted away.\\n+\\n+b) Complexity calibration\\n+- Counts are raised to 5-6 teal and 5-6 red frames, so each sample has 10-12 total.\\n+  That is enough to make 3-4 stacking layers appear without making the puzzle depend\\n+  on a huge number of object categories.\\n+- The generator uses only a small row-pattern vocabulary per side. Every sample has\\n+  repeated exact rows to prove the containment-triggered stack, and also has at least\\n+  one partial-overlap row offset by 2 or 3 to prove that partial overlap no longer\\n+  causes shifting.\\n+- I keep the original code/spec ordering rule based on input columns, not the loose\\n+  prose summary above. That keeps the puzzle anchored to the source task instead of\\n+  silently changing the sorting axis too.\\n+\\n+c) Implicit-assumption audit of `main()`\\n+- Scanning 5x5 windows for monochrome borders still works because the generator keeps\\n+  the input squares non-overlapping and axis-aligned.\\n+- The old `placed_at_default` list assumed only two output columns per side. That is\\n+  invalid once 10-12 frames can create 3-4 layers, so the new solver tracks occupied\\n+  row spans separately for every layer and chooses the shallowest legal one.\\n+- The old `overlaps(...)` predicate is invalid for D11/0. It is replaced by a\\n+  symmetric \\\"fully nested\\\" test. With fixed 5x5 squares this means equal row spans,\\n+  but the implementation keeps the generic containment wording instead of hard-coding\\n+  equality.\\n+- `paste(... transparent=bg)` is still safe. The input generator never overlaps input\\n+  frames, while output overdraw is intentional and comes only from the stacking rule.\\n+- New solver assumption: the number of required layers must fit in the grid width.\\n+  The generator caps the designed depth at 4, and width 42 gives columns\\n+  `0,5,10,15` on the left and `37,32,27,22` on the right with a visible center gap.\\n+\\n+d) Placement legality\\n+- Input placement is guaranteed by construction. Teal and red use disjoint row bands,\\n+  and each side uses fixed monotone input columns spaced by exactly 5, so no two\\n+  input 5x5 frames